{
  "abstract": "For a finite family of differentiable convex beta-smooth losses with a population minimizer, this formalization proves an expected last-iterate population-loss bound for stochastic gradient descent under uniform sampling with replacement and uniform sampling without replacement. With delta = min(1, 2 - eta beta), the bound is 280000 times log(T+2)^3 / log(2+log(T+2)) times [R^2 / (delta^3 eta T) + eta sigma_star^2 / delta^4]. It holds for all 0 < eta beta < 2, every positive WR horizon, and every WOR horizon through one full pass, including T = n. Under interpolation at eta = 1/beta, this yields O(beta R^2 log(T)^3 / (T log log(T))). The two compared theorems use the actual finite sampling averages and the usual SGD recursion. All history estimates and stochastic sampling identities are proved from the stated assumptions.",
  "authors": [
    {
      "name": "Yimin Zhong"
    }
